[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Under the Hood of Vue 3 Reactivity (Proxies and Effects)"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Vue 3 Reactivity"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Vue 3 overhauled its reactivity system, replacing the ES5 Object.defineProperty() API with ES6 Proxies. This shift solved long-standing limitations like the inability to detect property additions or deletions, delivering a faster and more robust foundation for modern web applications."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"How ES6 Proxies Work"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Proxy object wraps another object and intercepts fundamental operations like property lookup, assignment, enumeration, and function invocation. Instead of mutating or redefining object properties directly, Vue 3 wraps your state objects in a Proxy to monitor when properties are read (get) or written (set)."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"const state = { count: 0 };
const proxyState = new Proxy(state, {
  get(target, key) {
    console.log("Property accessed:", key);
    return target[key];
  },
  set(target, key, value) {
    console.log("Property modified:", key, "to", value);
    target[key] = value;
    return true;
  }
});

proxyState.count = 1; // Triggers 'set' trap"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"The Role of Effects and Track/Trigger"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reactivity requires two core components: tracking when a piece of state is read, and triggering updates when that state changes. Vue uses the concept of an 'effect'—a function that automatically re-runs when its dependent reactive properties change."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Track: Occurs inside the 'get' trap. Vue registers the current active effect as a subscriber to the accessed property."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Trigger: Occurs inside the 'set' trap. Vue looks up all subscribed effects for the modified property and schedules them to run again."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Building a Minimal Reactivity Engine"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To truly understand Vue 3 internals, we can write a simplified version of the reactivity core using a WeakMap, a Set, and Proxies."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"let activeEffect = null;
const targetMap = new WeakMap();

function track(target, key) {
  if (activeEffect) {
    let depsMap = targetMap.get(target);
    if (!depsMap) {
      targetMap.set(target, (depsMap = new Map()));
    }
    let dep = depsMap.get(key);
    if (!dep) {
      depsMap.set(key, (dep = new Set()));
    }
    dep.add(activeEffect);
  }
}

function trigger(target, key) {
  const depsMap = targetMap.get(target);
  if (!depsMap) return;
  const dep = depsMap.get(key);
  if (dep) {
    dep.forEach(effect => effect());
  }
}

function reactive(target) {
  return new Proxy(target, {
    get(target, key) {
      track(target, key);
      return target[key];
    },
    set(target, key, value) {
      target[key] = value;
      trigger(target, key);
      return true;
    }
  });
}

function effect(fn) {
  activeEffect = fn;
  fn();
  activeEffect = null;
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Putting It All Together"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Using our custom mini-engine, we can declare a reactive object and wrap a side-effect function. Notice how modifying the property automatically invokes the effect."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"const user = reactive({ name: "Alice" });

effect(() => {
  console.log("Effect executed, name is:", user.name);
});

// Output: Effect executed, name is: Alice

user.name = "Bob";
// Output: Effect executed, name is: Bob"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Vue 3 leverages ES6 Proxies combined with precise track and trigger mechanisms to achieve fine-grained reactivity. Understanding this underlying mechanics helps you write more performant code, avoid common pitfalls, and debug complex component re-renders with ease."}]
